.Dd September 24, 2026
.Dt MKBOM 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm mkbom
.Nd create a bill of materials (BOM) for a directory tree
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl s
.Ar directory
.Ar bomFile
.Nm
.Op Fl s
.Fl i Ar fileList
.Ar bomFile
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility creates a bill of materials file
.Ar bomFile
that describes the directory tree rooted at
.Ar directory .
A BOM is a binary container used during installation to record the
files, directories, symbolic links, device nodes, and other entries
that a package is expected to install.
.Pp
The directory tree is scanned recursively.
Entries are written to the BOM in the order in which they are
encountered.
Hard-linked files are recorded once as a hierarchy of hard links so
that they can be re-created as a hard-link group on installation,
while symbolic links are stored with their target path.
.Pp
The BOM written by
.Nm
is byte-for-byte identical to the corresponding output of Apple's
mkbom for ordinary directory trees.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Fl
.It Fl i Ar fileList
Read an
.Xr lsbom 1
listing from
.Ar fileList
instead of scanning a directory tree and write the resulting BOM to
.Ar bomFile .
.It Fl s
Write only minimal path records, omitting checksums and per-file
contents data.  Combined with
.Fl i ,
the on-disk layout is functional but not byte-identical to Apple's.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr lsbom 1 ,
.Xr bom 5
.Sh HISTORY
This implementation is a clean-room rewrite of the classic
.Nm
utility.
By default it walks a directory; simplified mode
.Pq Fl s
emits bare path records, and file-list mode
.Pq Fl i
builds a BOM from an
.Xr lsbom 1
listing.
The
.Fl s
and
.Fl i
options can be combined; that combination does not reproduce
Apple's exact on-disk layout but is otherwise functional.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An LibreDarwin